.UPDATE...
Issued at 943 AM PDT Sun Jul 26 2026

Minor updates to the forecast package this morning with the main
emphasis on temperatures, as well as heat and fire weather
messaging. Despite 24 hour temperature differences running 2-5
degrees lower than this time yesterday, the relatively dry airmass
should promote a sharp diurnal temperature curve mainly away from
the coast. Highs across the interior should climb into the 90s
with a few locales topping the century mark, especially across the
interior Central Coast and this has been reflected in the latest
update.

Elevated breezes, especially across some of the favored gaps and
passes, will approach the 30 to 40 mph range. Despite the flow
being onshore, the shallow marine layer may not provide much of a
boost to the afternoon humidity across these interior regions.
Fuels continue to cure and as a result, there will be an elevated
fire weather threat today and potentially through mid-week for the
interior North Bay, East Bay, and Central Coast. Additionally,
overnight RH recovery through Wednesday morning may only range
between 25 and 50% above 2500` elevation across the North Bay
Mountains, Santa Lucia, and Diablo Ranges. Be sure to avoid
activities that could spark a fire, especially across the interior
Central Coast, where vegetation dryness is the greatest. We`ll
continue to examine the potential for above normal temperatures
later this week as a ridge over the Pacific Southwest attempts to
build westward. It certainly appears that locations across the
East Bay and Central Coast interior will have the opportunity for
impactful heat as early as Thursday.


