.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1232 AM PDT Sun Jul 26 2026
(Today and tonight)

Upper level troughing holds over the eastern Pacific and into the
PacNW today, slowly meandering eastward. Satellite trends show
stratus forming along portions of the northern California coast,
which should bring stratus to most coastal locations and down into
the Monterey Bay. Uncertainty remains on how deep the marine layer
gets tonight, though current thinking is it`ll be between 500-
1000ft. Any stratus that forms tonight should retreat to the coast
by mid to late morning with mainly sunny skies prevailing.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1232 AM PDT Sun Jul 26 2026
(Monday through Saturday)

We will remain under an elongated trough through early to midweek,
leading to typical summer like days. Look for seasonal temperatures,
breezy onshore winds each afternoon and evening, and the return of
stratus. The marine layer should get to about 1000ft for the next
few nights, with it a 500-1000ft marine layer by midweek for those
along the coast. This is when we start to see high pressure over the
Desert Soutwest build into southern and central California and begin
pushing the trough and making it a bit more compact. Onshore flow
should help moderate temperatures near the coast, as we go from a
trough to quasi-zonal flow. Warmer temperatures will be expected
inland, but overall temperatures will continue to increase as we
head into the weekend. Widespread Moderate HeatRisk continues to
look likely for interior locations.

