.AVIATION...
(12Z TAFS)
Issued at 535 AM PDT Sun Jul 26 2026

Marine stratus is more organized than the past couple of nights.
Our presently ascending weather balloon indicates that marine
layer depth is around 1200-1500 feet, allowing for an influx of
stratus as far east as the LVK. Area webcams do indicate that this
deck of MVFR to IFR ceilings is thin and as such I anticipate that
after sunrise, clouds will erode. Visibility reductions are
anticipated to be limited to STS where river valley fog has
arrived and occasionally at HAF. By mid to late morning, VFR is
anticipated to return to most terminals, except HAF. VFR is
advertised here after 21Z with low to medium confidence. Monday
morning should feature a slightly stronger onshore push. Model
performance over the last few days does lower confidence in the
stratus forecast and if afternoon satellite trends are similar to
the past few days, alterations may be needed.

Vicinity of SFO...Intermittent IFR and MVFR anticipated to persist
through the morning push, but high confidence in VFR by 18Z.
Onshore flow through the San Bruno Gap coupled with a slightly
compressed marine layer will support wind gusts to near 30 knots.
There is an outside chance that gusts over 35 knots could
transpire. By sunrise Monday, stratus is anticipated to return as
onshore flow is expected to increase slightly (tighter SFO-SAC/SMX
gradient), though confidence is low in the onset time. Model
guidance offers a broad range with the earliest time around 05Z.
Overall, the model consensus is closer to 09Z and this timing
would be earlier than the previous nights, lending a bit of
confidence in this being a reasonable scenario. With the stronger
onshore push, MVFR is advertised through the extended portion of
the TAF, but additional refinements are probable.

SFO Bridge Approach...Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals...Stratus has been slow to materialize this
morning, but latest satellite imagery indicates a building deck of
clouds along the bay. IFR, with intermittent VFR should be
anticipated, especially at MRY through late morning. At SNS, IFR
is expected to be more intermittent in nature and should arrive
with the next hour. Tonight, a more typical stratus pattern should
set up with MVFR/IFR returning between 02-04Z with IFR at MRY. IFR
is a little less certain at SNS, so for now MVFR is forecast.

