.BEACHES...
Issued at 911 AM PDT Sat Jul 25 2026

Tropical cyclones in the Eastern Pacific are generating a long-
period southwesterly swell which has reached the coastline of the
San Francisco Bay Area and Central Coast. As such, sneaker waves and
strong rip currents are the primary concerns. However, breaking
waves can reach up to 10 feet along some south and southwest facing
beaches and break points (like Santa Cruz, Pacifica, and parts of
Stinson or Ocean Beach). Sneaker waves can have 20+ minutes in
between the larger sets (breaking waves) with significant "flat/calm
looking" times between them. This can potentially catch beachgoers
by surprise and resulting in people being swept into the cold
turbulent ocean; hence the name "sneaker". Rip currents are
strong enough to pull the strongest swimmers away from shore. Swim
near a lifeguard if possible. Stay back from the beach, and away
from jetties, piers, beachside rocks, and other infrastructure
near the water. Never turn your back to the ocean! The Beach
Hazards Statement has been extended through Wednesday afternoon
for all beaches along the Pacific Coast.

